This memoised function `ways(n)`, whose argument n is a positive whole number, performs Θ(n) additions (a number proportional to n, ignoring their growing bit cost). Yet calling `ways(5000)` on a fresh interpreter raises `RecursionError`. Why, and what is a good fix?

```python
from functools import lru_cache

# Cache completed calls by argument.
@lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def ways(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return 1
    return ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2)
```

